  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nAn upper level extratropical cyclone seen lifting north-northeast on \nGOES water vapor imagery loops has begun a more meridional advection \nbefore it is expected to slide east across the International Border \nwith Minnesota, dragging an appendage of mid level vorticity \nadvection across the Upper Mississippi River Valley. Resultant \nwidespread precipitation chances increase from west to east from the \nlate evening into the overnight hours.\n\nStorm Chances Today:\n\nPeak diurnal heating concurrent with location of strongest WAA \nprecedent of the frontal boundary remains slightly upstream of the \nforecast area, keeping highest (50%) thunder probabilities upstream. \nStrongest low level winds raise higher severe threat in these areas, \ngrazing the western parts of the local county warning area from \nsoutheast Minnesota into west-central Wisconsin. All hazard types \nwill be possible based on long-skinny CAPE profiles resulting in a \nlow CAPE, high Shear environment should storms be able to initialize \nin the less hospitable \"October\" airmass. Local forecast area will \nlikely see storms, question of strength and intensity remains in \nquestion.\n\nWarm Today:\n\nThe local airmass will be more resemblant of a late-September \nairmass with daytime high temperatures in the low to mid 70s, likely \nresulting in local climatological warm records being broken.\n\nPrecipitation Chances This Weekend & Early Next Week:\n\nPersistently low confident long term forecast remains the trend as \nprevious NBM PoPS early weekend have shunted south of the forecast \narea, with LREF agreement increasing for cyclogenesis farther south \nthrough the central CONUS. Therefore, higher PoPs now pushed into \nMonday through Wednesday instead of through the weekend in 24 hour \nago forecast.",
